1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,115
Pasta looks like the easiest food in the

2
00:00:02,125 --> 00:00:04,922
world, so why does it end up bland,

3
00:00:05,097 --> 00:00:06,861
sticky or mushy?

4
00:00:07,825 --> 00:00:10,460
A few small habits change everything.

5
00:00:10,820 --> 00:00:14,976
Seven steps: the right salt, the right
timing,

6
00:00:15,151 --> 00:00:16,996
and a sauce that clings.

7
00:00:17,961 --> 00:00:22,302
Stay for step five: the ingredient most
people pour away.

8
00:00:22,933 --> 00:00:23,826
WikiTube.

9
00:00:24,059 --> 00:00:26,473
Whatever it is, here's how.

10
00:00:26,767 --> 00:00:29,471
Good pasta isn't about fancy ingredients.

11
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:34,117
It's technique: plenty of water, enough
salt, stopping

12
00:00:34,127 --> 00:00:36,857
at the right moment, and finishing the
pasta

13
00:00:36,867 --> 00:00:40,222
in the sauce instead of pouring sauce on
top.

14
00:00:40,675 --> 00:00:43,440
It takes no extra time, and it works

15
00:00:43,450 --> 00:00:45,957
for almost every dried pasta shape.

16
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,288
You'll need dried pasta, about a quarter
pound

17
00:00:49,298 --> 00:00:53,456
per person, a large pot, coarse salt, a

18
00:00:53,466 --> 00:00:56,229
colander, and your favorite sauce.

19
00:00:56,462 --> 00:00:59,096
Keep a mug nearby for the pasta water.

20
00:00:59,633 --> 00:01:00,700
Step one.

21
00:01:01,154 --> 00:01:03,823
Fill a large pot with plenty of water,

22
00:01:03,998 --> 00:01:06,809
roughly four to five litres, or about a

23
00:01:06,819 --> 00:01:09,790
gallon, for every pound of pasta.

24
00:01:10,337 --> 00:01:13,345
This lets the pasta move freely, and stops

25
00:01:13,355 --> 00:01:15,795
the water temperature from dropping too
much when

26
00:01:15,805 --> 00:01:16,652
you add it.

27
00:01:17,802 --> 00:01:20,160
Cooking pasta in a small pot makes it

28
00:01:20,170 --> 00:01:22,178
clump and turn sticky.

29
00:01:22,725 --> 00:01:25,234
Put the pot on the largest burner, cover

30
00:01:25,244 --> 00:01:27,219
it with a lid, and bring it up

31
00:01:27,229 --> 00:01:30,014
to a rolling boil, which is faster.

32
00:01:30,468 --> 00:01:33,405
A tight lid helps the water boil faster,

33
00:01:33,498 --> 00:01:35,726
which saves time and energy.

34
00:01:36,087 --> 00:01:38,374
Remove it once the pasta goes in.

35
00:01:38,933 --> 00:01:39,989
Step two.

36
00:01:40,536 --> 00:01:43,530
Once the water is boiling, add salt.

37
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:46,528
A good rule is about one to two

38
00:01:46,538 --> 00:01:49,686
tablespoons of coarse salt for four
litres, or

39
00:01:49,696 --> 00:01:51,274
a gallon, of water.

40
00:01:51,936 --> 00:01:54,281
It should taste like mild seawater.

41
00:01:54,827 --> 00:01:56,872
This is the only chance to season the

42
00:01:56,882 --> 00:01:59,995
pasta itself from the inside, and salt in

43
00:02:00,005 --> 00:02:02,721
the water won't make the pasta too salty,

44
00:02:02,896 --> 00:02:05,229
because most of it goes down the drain.

45
00:02:05,891 --> 00:02:10,569
Under-salted water is the number one
reason pasta tastes flat.

46
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:13,553
Don't be shy about the salt.

47
00:02:13,914 --> 00:02:15,994
Most of it stays in the water, and

48
00:02:16,004 --> 00:02:18,673
the pasta absorbs only a small amount.

49
00:02:19,475 --> 00:02:21,297
Now the pasta can go in.

50
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:22,819
Step three.

51
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:25,747
Add the pasta to the boiling water, and

52
00:02:25,757 --> 00:02:27,814
push it under the surface with a wooden

53
00:02:27,824 --> 00:02:29,588
spoon as it softens.

54
00:02:30,738 --> 00:02:33,480
Stir well during the first two minutes,
because

55
00:02:33,490 --> 00:02:36,278
that's when the pasta is stickiest, and it

56
00:02:36,288 --> 00:02:37,425
can clump together.

57
00:02:38,087 --> 00:02:39,828
Don't add oil to the water.

58
00:02:40,189 --> 00:02:42,721
It doesn't prevent sticking, and it makes
the

59
00:02:42,731 --> 00:02:45,099
sauce slide off the pasta later.

60
00:02:46,063 --> 00:02:48,433
Keep the water at a steady boil, with

61
00:02:48,443 --> 00:02:51,182
the lid off, so it doesn't boil over.

62
00:02:52,333 --> 00:02:54,877
Long pasta like spaghetti will soften in a

63
00:02:54,887 --> 00:02:57,617
minute, so just keep pushing it down until

64
00:02:57,627 --> 00:02:59,321
it is fully under water.

65
00:02:59,867 --> 00:03:00,923
Step four.

66
00:03:01,470 --> 00:03:04,072
Check the cooking time on the package,
then

67
00:03:04,082 --> 00:03:06,879
set a timer for one or two minutes less.

68
00:03:07,425 --> 00:03:09,166
Start tasting then.

69
00:03:09,619 --> 00:03:12,048
The pasta should be al dente, which means

70
00:03:12,058 --> 00:03:14,103
soft but with a slight firmness in the

71
00:03:14,113 --> 00:03:15,493
center when you bite it.

72
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:18,166
Fish out a piece with a fork, let

73
00:03:18,176 --> 00:03:20,625
it cool for a moment, and bite it.

74
00:03:21,287 --> 00:03:22,787
If you see a white dot in the

75
00:03:22,797 --> 00:03:24,944
middle, it needs another minute.

76
00:03:25,607 --> 00:03:28,636
Tasting is more reliable than timing
alone.

77
00:03:29,786 --> 00:03:33,445
Different brands and shapes cook
differently, so always

78
00:03:33,455 --> 00:03:35,767
trust your own bite more than the number

79
00:03:35,777 --> 00:03:36,751
on the box.

80
00:03:37,902 --> 00:03:41,534
But before you drain, there is one thing
to save.

81
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:43,167
Step five.

82
00:03:43,621 --> 00:03:46,026
Before you drain anything, scoop out a mug

83
00:03:46,036 --> 00:03:47,718
of the cloudy cooking water.

84
00:03:48,009 --> 00:03:50,565
It's full of starch and salt, and it's

85
00:03:50,575 --> 00:03:53,906
the secret ingredient of restaurant pasta
sauces.

86
00:03:54,569 --> 00:03:57,403
A few spoonfuls will thicken your sauce,
help

87
00:03:57,413 --> 00:04:00,152
it cling to the pasta and make it silky.

88
00:04:01,117 --> 00:04:03,382
Set the mug next to the stove, where

89
00:04:03,392 --> 00:04:04,552
you won't forget it.

90
00:04:05,215 --> 00:04:07,724
You can pour out any extra later, but

91
00:04:07,734 --> 00:04:09,779
you can't get it back once the pot

92
00:04:09,789 --> 00:04:10,833
is in the sink.

93
00:04:11,194 --> 00:04:13,494
You can also stir a spoonful into soups

94
00:04:13,504 --> 00:04:16,336
and stews later, to give them body.

95
00:04:16,900 --> 00:04:17,979
Step six.

96
00:04:19,129 --> 00:04:21,683
Drain the pasta in a colander in the sink.

97
00:04:22,485 --> 00:04:23,993
Be careful of the steam.

98
00:04:24,354 --> 00:04:26,712
Shake it briefly, but don't rinse it with

99
00:04:26,722 --> 00:04:29,475
water, which washes off the starch that
helps

100
00:04:29,485 --> 00:04:30,599
the sauce stick.

101
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:34,175
The only exception is a cold pasta salad.

102
00:04:34,977 --> 00:04:38,006
Don't let the drained pasta sit and cool,

103
00:04:38,030 --> 00:04:39,272
since it clumps.

104
00:04:39,725 --> 00:04:42,026
Move straight to the next step, with the

105
00:04:42,036 --> 00:04:46,726
sauce already warm in a wide pan, ready
and waiting.

106
00:04:47,690 --> 00:04:50,258
Pasta is best eaten hot, so have your

107
00:04:50,268 --> 00:04:53,114
plates, your cheese and your sauce all
ready

108
00:04:53,124 --> 00:04:54,330
before you drain.

109
00:04:55,295 --> 00:04:58,103
Now for the step that brings it all
together.

110
00:04:58,667 --> 00:04:59,746
Step seven.

111
00:05:00,409 --> 00:05:02,430
Tip the pasta into the pan with your

112
00:05:02,440 --> 00:05:06,061
warm sauce, and add a splash of the pasta
water.

113
00:05:06,724 --> 00:05:09,350
Stir or toss over medium heat for one

114
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:12,194
to two minutes, until the sauce coats
every

115
00:05:12,204 --> 00:05:15,524
piece, and the pasta finishes cooking in
the flavor.

116
00:05:15,884 --> 00:05:18,298
Add more pasta water if it looks dry.

117
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:22,083
Take the pan off the heat, add cheese,

118
00:05:22,107 --> 00:05:24,823
fresh herbs or a drizzle of olive oil,

119
00:05:24,917 --> 00:05:28,030
and serve right away on warm plates,
because

120
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:29,780
pasta waits for no one.

121
00:05:30,141 --> 00:05:32,627
If you are making baked pasta, stop one

122
00:05:32,637 --> 00:05:34,798
or two minutes early, because it will cook

123
00:05:34,808 --> 00:05:35,968
more in the oven.

124
00:05:36,533 --> 00:05:39,261
Most pasta problems have a simple fix.

125
00:05:39,621 --> 00:05:41,875
If the pasta is stuck together, toss it

126
00:05:41,885 --> 00:05:43,663
in the hot sauce with a splash of

127
00:05:43,673 --> 00:05:46,331
pasta water, and it will loosen.

128
00:05:47,133 --> 00:05:49,317
If the sauce is too thin, simmer it

129
00:05:49,327 --> 00:05:51,288
with the pasta for another minute.

130
00:05:51,742 --> 00:05:53,961
If it tastes flat, add a pinch of

131
00:05:53,971 --> 00:05:55,584
salt or some cheese.

132
00:05:56,386 --> 00:05:59,615
Overcooked pasta cannot be saved, so use
it

133
00:05:59,625 --> 00:06:01,621
in a baked dish or soup.

134
00:06:02,167 --> 00:06:04,592
The right shape makes a better dish.

135
00:06:05,394 --> 00:06:09,459
Long, thin pasta like spaghetti suits
light, smooth

136
00:06:09,469 --> 00:06:11,767
sauces with oil or tomato.

137
00:06:12,314 --> 00:06:15,589
Short shapes with ridges or hollows, like
penne

138
00:06:15,599 --> 00:06:19,139
or rigatoni, catch thick, chunky sauces.

139
00:06:19,941 --> 00:06:23,180
Wide ribbons suit rich, creamy sauces.

140
00:06:24,330 --> 00:06:27,013
Fresh pasta cooks in only a few minutes,

141
00:06:27,023 --> 00:06:29,414
so stay by the pot and taste early.

142
00:06:29,868 --> 00:06:32,921
Whole wheat and gluten-free pasta vary a
lot,

143
00:06:33,049 --> 00:06:35,661
so follow the package and taste sooner.

144
00:06:36,200 --> 00:06:39,055
Leftover pasta keeps well if you cool it
quickly.

145
00:06:39,509 --> 00:06:41,658
Within two hours of cooking, put it in

146
00:06:41,668 --> 00:06:43,095
a container in the fridge.

147
00:06:43,386 --> 00:06:45,780
Toss it with a teaspoon of oil first,

148
00:06:45,790 --> 00:06:47,983
so it does not set into one block.

149
00:06:49,133 --> 00:06:51,245
Eat it within three to four days.

150
00:06:51,699 --> 00:06:54,313
To reheat, add a splash of water, and

151
00:06:54,323 --> 00:06:56,205
warm it in a pan or covered in

152
00:06:56,215 --> 00:06:58,513
the microwave until steaming hot.

153
00:06:59,663 --> 00:07:02,635
Plain cooked pasta also freezes well.

154
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:05,173
Here are three mistakes to avoid.

155
00:07:05,626 --> 00:07:09,015
First, too little water or too little
salt.

156
00:07:09,307 --> 00:07:11,802
That's what makes pasta sticky and bland.

157
00:07:12,604 --> 00:07:17,003
Second, rinsing the pasta, or adding oil
to the water.

158
00:07:18,153 --> 00:07:20,439
Both stop the sauce from clinging.

159
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:23,168
And third, overcooking.

160
00:07:24,132 --> 00:07:26,978
Pasta continues to soften in the sauce, so

161
00:07:26,988 --> 00:07:29,170
take it out while it still has a bite.

162
00:07:30,321 --> 00:07:33,236
Fix these three things, and your weeknight
pasta

163
00:07:33,246 --> 00:07:36,264
will taste like it came from a good
restaurant.

164
00:07:37,228 --> 00:07:40,327
Pasta is forgiving when you respect the
basics,

165
00:07:40,352 --> 00:07:43,450
so stay relaxed and keep tasting as you
go.

166
00:07:43,997 --> 00:07:48,489
Put these habits together, and good pasta
becomes automatic.

167
00:07:49,033 --> 00:07:50,158
Let's recap.

168
00:07:50,449 --> 00:07:52,259
Use a big pot of water.

169
00:07:52,922 --> 00:07:54,082
Salt it well.

170
00:07:54,745 --> 00:07:57,403
Add the pasta and stir at the start.

171
00:07:58,205 --> 00:08:00,445
Time it, and taste.

172
00:08:01,247 --> 00:08:03,185
Save a mug of pasta water.

173
00:08:04,335 --> 00:08:06,308
Drain, but don't rinse.

174
00:08:06,436 --> 00:08:08,665
And finish the pasta in the sauce.

175
00:08:09,026 --> 00:08:12,464
Seven steps, about fifteen minutes, and
pasta that

176
00:08:12,474 --> 00:08:14,028
tastes the way it should.

177
00:08:15,179 --> 00:08:17,886
Bonus sections cover how to fix common
pasta

178
00:08:17,896 --> 00:08:20,858
problems, how to match shape to sauce, and

179
00:08:20,868 --> 00:08:22,620
how to store leftovers.

180
00:08:23,167 --> 00:08:26,649
That's how you cook pasta properly, with
salt,

181
00:08:26,824 --> 00:08:28,971
timing and sauce.

182
00:08:29,517 --> 00:08:33,246
If dinner tastes better tonight, subscribe
to WikiTube,

183
00:08:33,256 --> 00:08:36,738
because we publish a new how-to every
single day.

184
00:08:37,099 --> 00:08:39,086
Tell us in the comments what your favorite

185
00:08:39,096 --> 00:08:41,304
pasta shape is, and what you would like

186
00:08:41,314 --> 00:08:42,834
us to explain next.

187
00:08:43,984 --> 00:08:45,144
See you tomorrow.

188
00:08:45,505 --> 00:08:48,037
Next time, try adding a pinch of chili

189
00:08:48,047 --> 00:08:49,277
to your sauce.

190
00:08:49,367 --> 00:08:50,585
